{
  "items": [
    {
      "id": "a01",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 3,
      "estimated_minutes": 3,
      "reason": "One rereading is needed to compare the description's non-contract-claim coverage question with d01's reliance on the agreement's substantive duties, particularly the repackaged tort claims in line 49. The federal-court-designation objection is plainly separate and requires no factual investigation to assess this call."
    },
    {
      "id": "a02",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 3,
      "estimated_minutes": 3,
      "reason": "The reviewer needs a brief comparison of the tort, trade-secret, and non-compete coverage language with d02's substantive-duty analysis. The parallel Lease clause is readily checked, and the additional question about which federal court is meant is separable from that comparison."
    },
    {
      "id": "a03",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 1,
      "estimated_minutes": 1,
      "reason": "The description expressly targets the identity of the federal forum and whether selection is mandatory; d03 concerns which claims enforce contractual duties. Those subjects are immediately distinguishable without researching federal court organization."
    },
    {
      "id": "a04",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 1,
      "estimated_minutes": 1,
      "reason": "The reviewer can distinguish the description's forum-identity and exclusivity objections from d04's claim-coverage issue at a glance. No examination of the agreement's substantive obligations is needed to identify what this description alleges."
    },
    {
      "id": "a05",
      "difficulty": "hard",
      "score": 7,
      "estimated_minutes": 12,
      "reason": "The work is deciding what the description names: uncertainty about which documents are 'related agreements,' or the reach of 'relating to' over non-contract claims. Its final reference to non-contract claims leaves an interpretive judgment about whether it reaches d05's claim-to-agreement connection problem, while d06 addresses closely overlapping language."
    },
    {
      "id": "a06",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 3,
      "estimated_minutes": 3,
      "reason": "A brief check of Section 7.7 establishes the asserted integration-clause tension and permits comparison with all three defects. That alleged conflict is specifically identified, making it straightforward to distinguish from patent-claim relevance under d05 and d06 and the jury-waiver language under d07."
    }
  ]
}
